rm-russia-of-ukrainian-military-activities"},"https://www.dw.com/en/india-news-9-killed-after-bus-catches-fire-outside-delhi/live-79405879?maca=en-rss-en-all-1573-xml-mrss":{"fetched_utc":"2026-09-24T10:55:45Z","status":"ok","text":"Skip to content Skip to main menu Skip to more DW sites https://p.dw.com/p/5NB5z Police said the fire started in the AC unit of the bus on Wednesday Image: IANS Advertisement Skip next section What you need to know What you need to know Fire on sleeper bus kills nine outside of Delhi This is the latest in similar incidents of deadly fires on buses SIT files chargesheet in Ayodhya Ram Temple donation theft case Three arrested over alleged gang rape of minor girl in New Delhi Here are some of the major headlines from India on Thursday, September 24: Tired of missing our real-time updates? Click here to add us as a Preferred Source on Google. Then tap the \"Star\" or \"Preferred\" to keep DW News at the top of your feed. Skip next section Three arrested over alleged gang rape of 17-year-old girl in New Delhi 09/24/2026 September 24, 2026 Three arrested over alleged gang rape of 17-year-old girl in New Delhi Authorities have arrested three men accused gang-raping a 17-year-old girl in southeast Delhi. The girl had been visiting a temple with a male friend on Monday evening before the two went to a nearby park, where the suspects allegedly intercepted them. The men posed as police officers to intimidate the minor and her friend. One of the men reportedly restrained the girl's friend while the other two assaulted her. The suspects allegedly threatened the pair with arrest if they reported the incident, according to police cited by The Hindustan Times . On Wednesday, ​hundreds of students protested in Delhi, demanding better safety for women. The alleged assault occurred barely 2 km (1.2 miles) from Lady Shri Ram (LSR) College, one of the country's top women's colleges. India has a high rate of reported sexual violence against women and girls. National Crime Records Bureau data shows that more than 80 rape cases are reported to police across the country each day. https://p.dw.com/p/5NBtY Skip next section SIT files chargesheet in Ayodhya Ram Temple donation theft case 09/24/2026 September 24, 2026 SIT files chargesheet in Ayodhya Ram Temple donation theft case The Ram Temple became embroiled in controversy in recent months following allegations of theft involving donations and offerings worth millions of rupees [FILE: January 22, 2024] Image: Wikimedia An investigation team probing the alleged embezzlement of donations and offerings at the Ram Temple in the northern city of Ayodhya has filed a chargesheet against eight arrested suspects, media reports said on Wednesday. The police filed a 74,000-page chargesheet before a local court. All eight accused are in judicial custody. The Special Investigation Team's (SIT) status report documented 105 instances of theft captured by CCTV cameras at the temple premises. The controversy over alleged embezzlement forced authorities to appoint an Indian Air Force veteran, Jeetendra Mishra, as the chief executive officer of the temple earlier this month. The construction of the temple, inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi in January 2024, was a longstanding political issue and a key commitment of Modi's nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP). It built at the site of the Babri mosque, which was demolished by Hindu nationalist mobs in 1992. Devout Hindus believe that the god Ram was born there more than 7,000 years ago, but that the Babri mosque was built over his birthplace by a 16th-century Muslim emperor. https://p.dw.com/p/5NBdy Skip next section Fire on sleeper bus kills nine outside of Delhi 09/24/2026 September 24, 2026 Fire on sleeper bus kills nine outside of Delhi Nine passengers were killed late on Wednesday after an overnight sleeper bus caught fire outside of the national capital, New Delhi, according to media reports. A police report, cited by the media, said the bus' air conditioning unit caused the fire while on the Yamuna Expressway in Greater Noida. Police added that the bus driver and conductor managed to escape the fire and are detained by the authorities, according to The Times of India newspaper. A passenger who managed to escape the tragedy told ANI news agency that the bus was packed with passengers. \"I was sleeping when someone woke me up saying the bus had caught fire, and we all scrambled to get out,\" the passenger said. \"It was not an easy situation to escape, there was a lot of smoke. We somehow managed to get out.\" The police are investigating the incident. This is the latest in similar incidents of deadly fires on buses: March 2026: At least 13 people died in a bus accident in Andhra Pradesh October 2025: At least 20 people died after a bus caught fire following a collision with a motorcycle in Kurnool, Andhra Pradesh October 2025: A bus fire in Rajasthan killed at least 20 people and left around 16 others seriously injured April 2025: At least 25 people died and eight others were injured after the bus they were traveling in caught fire on an expressway in Maharashtra https://p.dw.com/p/5NBAS Skip next section Welcome to our coverage 09/24/2026 September 24, 2026 Welcome to our coverage Hi, this is Shakeel from DW's New Delhi studio bringing you the latest updates from across India . Nine passengers were killed late on Wednesday when their overnight sleeper bus caught fire just outside Delhi. Meanwhile, a Special Investigation Team (SIT) filed a nearly 70,000-page chargesheet against eight people arrested in connection with the Ram Temple donation embezzlement case on Wednesday. For all this and more, follow our live blog . https://p.dw.com/p/5NB61 Show more posts Skip next section Similar stories Similar stories India news: Police investigate alleged gang-rape in Delhi A minor girl was allegedly gang-raped in a posh Delhi neighborhood, prompting local police to file a case. Crime 09/22/2026 September 22, 2026 India's road rage: When cars become weapons Road disputes are turning violent across India, raising questions about status and impunity behind the wheel. Society 09/21/2026 September 21, 2026 Modi says developing nations unfairly blamed for emissions PM Narendra Modi has told a climate conference that developed countries emit six times more CO2 than India. DW has more. Politics 09/20/2026 September 20, 2026 Show more Skip next section More on Politics from Asia More on Politics from Asia South Korea leans toward military conscription for women South Korea is facing a population decline and calls for social equality to be extended to national military service. Politics 08/29/2026 August 29, 2026 Should India worry about the Saudi-Pakistan-Turkey pact? A new defense pact is set to bring Islamabad deeper into the security architecture of the Middle East. Politics 08/18/2026 August 18, 2026 India: Anger over exam irregularities spreads to Jharkhand Young people in India's Jharkhand want a federal probe into alleged irregularities in civil service recruitment tests. Politics 08/10/2026 August 10, 2026 Show more Skip next section Related topics Related topics Manipur Narendra Modi Hindu nationalism India BRICS","chars":7004,"final_url":"https://www.dw.com/en/india-news-9-killed-after-bus-catches-fire-outside-delhi/live-79405879?maca=en-rss-en-all-1573-xml-mrss"},"https://www.forbes.ro/oana-ijdelea-managing-partner-ijdelea-associates-and-jennifer-fondrevay-founder-day1-ready-at-forbes-business-bridges-2026-complexity-can-be-managed-unpredictability-scares-inve-524830":{"fetched_utc":"2026-10-21T21:07:16Z","status":"ok","text":"Foreign investors can handle almost any legal complexity, as long as they can anticipate it. What drives them away from Romania is unpredictability: the different ways local authorities read and apply the same law. This diagnosis came from Oana Ijdelea, Managing Partner at Ijdelea & Associates, in her dialogue with Jennifer Fondrevay, Founder of Day1 Ready, at Forbes România Business Bridges 2026, the event organized by Forbes Romania at Forbes on Fifth in New York. \"The last five, actually six years show us that any resilient investor can deal with complexity. If you know your issues well enough, you are able to plan for them, manage them and adapt to them. However, when these problems turn into unpredictability, for example in how local regulators in Romania read and apply a certain legal provision, especially in real estate, these are the issues that generate future problems in operations and in investment implementation. For a certain type of risk-averse investor, it's simply a red flag, and it makes the investment a no-go,\" Oana Ijdelea said. The lawyer described a two-layer regulatory framework, national and European, with a local peculiarity. \"From our firm's experience, we see that the Romanian government tends to implement and actually overstep what is required by Brussels, with the good and the bad that comes from that. Usually it's the bad, but let's stay on the bright side of things,\" she observed. On top of this come foreign direct investment screening requirements, which every investor needs to understand early on. Jennifer Fondrevay, who specializes in the human side of mergers and acquisitions, moved the discussion from paper to people. \"The biggest challenge is remembering that we are humans. With the onslaught of technology and our quest for efficiency, we have forgotten that humans are fallible and that we also have emotions. In every merger, acquisition, business transformation or even AI adoption that I have seen, humans consistently have the same emotion when it comes to change: grieving the loss of the way things used to be,\" she explained. \"Humans hold on to how things were, to where they felt important and valued, and can struggle to pivot because they're afraid of not being good enough for what the future holds. The companies and leaders who recognize that and prepare their teams for it are the ones who succeed,\" Fondrevay said. The example she gave comes from Romania itself, where she worked with the UniCredit team on the changes brought by the bank's acquisitions. For Romanian-American deals, the consultant has two pieces of advice. The first: \"Think like a tourist. Not a bad tourist, we all know what a bad tourist looks like and acts like, but a good tourist who is curious, who asks questions.\" The second: invest time in the partnership. \"Invest time and energy into it. That's how you succeed, whether it's an American-Romanian partnership or any other,\" she said. For leaders going through a deal or a major transformation, Fondrevay gave an answer she herself described as disappointingly simple. \"The leaders who foster an environment for connection and collaboration are the ones who have a competitive advantage, particularly in the face of all the technology people are trying to embrace,\" she said. She also had a message addressed directly to Romanians: \"Romanians have a passion and a drive that is palpable. Don't lose that. The catching up has given you that spirit, and I think it's tremendous.\" \"Leaders must set the direction and the targets, and allow their organizations the flexibility to navigate an unwalked path towards those objectives. Some days the sun is up, some days it rains. Either way, you just have to be mentally prepared that the direction is there,\" Oana Ijdelea concluded.","chars":3786,"final_url":"https://www.forbes.ro/oana-ijdelea-managing-partner-ijdelea-associates-and-jennifer-fondrevay-founder-day1-ready-at-forbes-business-bridges-2026-complexity-can-be-managed-unpredictability-scares-inve-524830"}}